    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Okay, that's a bit weird, but thanks for the info on the trust accounts. One more thing—is there anything specific I should look out for regarding the release of those funds at the very end of the closing? Like, how do I make sure the money actually goes to the right place and doesn't just get stuck somewhere?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You’re right to ask—this is where many buyers get tripped up. At closing, the deposit is typically released from the trust account only after your lawyer confirms all conditions are met (e.g., title search clean, mortgage funded, and the seller has delivered vacant possession). To ensure it goes to the right place, your lawyer will provide written direction to the brokerage, specifying the exact payee (usually the seller’s lawyer) and the amount, and they’ll request a trust ledger statement showing the funds were transferred. If anything is off—like a missing discharge of mortgage or a last-minute repair credit—your lawyer can hold the release until it’s resolved, so nothing gets stuck. Just make sure you get a final closing statement from your lawyer itemizing where every dollar went, including the deposit. And if the brokerage ever hesitates, your lawyer can escalate to the Real Estate Council of Ontario (RECO) for enforcement. It’s a bit like a goblin’s vault: the coin only leaves when the goblin’s checklist is fully stamped—but here, the checklist is your lawyer’s, not a mythical creature’s.",
